-id "q-111468" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${d}_{2}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-111468/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111468"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:d