ion/id #uuid "5a3f3811-3d96-55cf-b222-418aca1a7192" :question/math-academy-id "q-111807" :question/problem "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is ${{r}_{1},{r}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-111807/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111807"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
